TAIL(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       TAIL(1)

NAME
    tail — Print the end of a file, because apparently the interesting
           stuff always happens at the bottom.

SYNOPSIS
    tail <file> [-n lines]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'tail' command prints the last few lines of a text file.

    By default, it displays the final 10 lines. Because unlike 'head',
    'tail' understands that the important part of a log is usually
    whatever happened five seconds ago.

    The number of lines can be customized with '-n'.

    The entire file is read into memory before the requested lines are
    selected. This is perfectly reasonable until you point it at a
    47-gigabyte log file.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file whose ending you wish to inspect.

        Required. 'tail' refuses to guess which file contains your
        latest disaster.

OPTIONS
    -n lines
        Number of lines to print.

        Defaults to 10.

        Increase it when 10 lines aren't enough to reconstruct the
        crime scene.

EXAMPLES
    > tail application.log
        Prints the last 10 lines of the log.

        Ah. There it is. The error.

    > tail -n 5 application.log
        Prints only the final 5 lines.

        Five lines should be enough to understand everything.

        It will not be enough.

    > tail -n 100 server.log
        Prints the last 100 lines.

        Now we're investigating.

    > tail README.md
        Prints the end of the README.

        Skipping straight to the conclusion. Respectable.

    > tail -n 0 enormous.log
        Prints nothing.

        You have successfully inspected the file without learning
        anything about it.

ERRORS
    tail error: <error>
        The file could not be opened or read.

        Possible causes include:
        - The file doesn't exist.
        - You don't have permission to read it.
        - The path is wrong.
        - The file has decided to become your problem.

USAGE ERRORS
    If the arguments cannot be parsed, 'tail' quietly exits.

    Because sometimes the best error message is simply an awkward
    silence.

OUTPUT
    The final requested number of lines are printed exactly as read.

    Files are interpreted as UTF-8 text. Invalid characters are replaced
    rather than causing the command to give up on life.

FUN FACTS
    - The default is 10 lines, just like 'head'.
    - Unlike traditional streaming implementations, this version reads
      the entire file before finding its tail.
    - File completion is enabled, because filenames are hard and tabs
      are wonderful.
    - 'tail' is particularly useful for logs, where the bottom of the
      file is often where the bodies are buried.
    - If the file has fewer than '-n' lines, congratulations: you get
      the whole thing for free.

